  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "C.P.Martin vs Food Inspector on 27 September, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 27Th Day Of September 2016/5Th Aswina, 1938 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1285 of 2012 () -------------------------------- Crl.A 22/2007 of Addl. Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Kalpetta Cc 95/2005 of J.M.F.C.-Ii, Sulthanbathery Revision Petitioner/Accused: ----------------------------------------------- C.P.Martin, Aged 41 Years S/O.Paul,Chirapparambil House,Kalady,Neeleswaram.P.O, Pin-673572. By Advs.Sri.M.A.Abdul Hakhim Sri.M.G.Anon Sri.Joseph George (Kannampuzha) Respondents/Complainant/Complainant & State : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Mananthavady Circle,Wynad District,Pin-683545. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By Public Prosecutor,High Court Of Kerala,Ernakulam,Pin-682031. Public Prosecutor, Shri.C.K.Prasad This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 27-09-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: C.P.Martin vs Food Inspector on 27 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/63764131/ 1 B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.1285 of 2012 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 27th day of September 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 14.10.2004 at about 2 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 750 m.l. of coconut oil from the petitioner. After complying with the legal formalities, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for coconut oil and hence, the sample was adulterated. It is also alleged that the revision petitioner did not have the requisite licence under the P.F.A. Rules to sell the food item. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P24(a) were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P13 report of the Public Analyst cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of C.P.Martin vs Food Inspector on 27 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/63764131/ 2 analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra).",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P13 report of the Public Analyst cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "6. It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of C.P.Martin vs Food Inspector on 27 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/63764131/ 2 analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra)."
  },
